{"... at Whitby the quality of play as far as the Eng­lish players were concerned was comparatively low this year and, as always, the end-game play in particular suffered badly. Take for example the following position which arose In the ninth round game between Ripley and De Veauce. From this position play went:} 31. f6+ Kd8 32. Nf5 Rxc4 {"And now White thought long and hard. Attracted by this I studied the board and came to the conclusion that he was hesitating between [the] two [following] continuations, both of which won out of hand."} 33. Nxd6 (33. f7 Nf8 34. Ra8+ Kd7 35. Rxf8 {, etc}) (33. Rxd7+ Kxd7 34. f7) 33... Rf4 34. f7 Rf6 35. Ra8+ Ke7 36. Nc4 Kxf7 37. Ra7 {"and eventually White won on the 50th move." Sources: The Times, 17 August 1968} 1-0

